{"reply":"The dossier is presented as a bench notebook by Maro Pell, dated September 2019. It is not described as a formal report or log. The dossier says the notebook follows 24 identified panels arranged in 4 shallow trays, with 6 panels in each tray, and that the observers photographed each identified sample beside the same square-grid reference card after three hours in each room. It does not give a detailed entry structure, page organization, timestamps, or more precise dating beyond September 2019 and the fact that part of the sequence occurred on the next day.

About the panels, the dossier says they are thin laminated test panels called foldglass by Corda workshop. They are experimental samples, not approved building components. Each sample carries an inked identifier that remains attached throughout the sequence. The dossier refers to laminated layers and proposes unequal moisture uptake across those layers as a possible mechanism, but it does not name the materials, give dimensions, describe the layup in detail, or say how the panels were made or obtained. It also states that no internal-layer microscopy was performed.

As for purpose and conclusion, the dossier describes the notebook as comparing the panels’ shape after movement between a damp room and a dry room. It does not give a final approved-product conclusion. It offers unequal moisture uptake across the laminated layers only as a working explanation, and it says room transfers alone do not rule out temperature drift or handling effects. It also warns that judging an edge from a photograph can depend on camera position. On repeated cycling and reversibility, it says the recorded sequence covers two days and supports reversibility only over that observed interval. It explicitly says the team did not test repeated cycling over several weeks."}

{"reply":"The dossier presents the document as a bench notebook by Tessa Rin, dated November 2018. It is not described as a formal report or a separate log. The dossier gives only limited structural information: it follows 32 reedleaf panels arranged in four shallow trays of 8 panels each, and the recorded sequence covers two days, including a next-day damp-to-dry-to-damp test of 6 responsive panels. It does not provide a more detailed entry format, such as numbered entries, page headings, or daily log headings.

About the panels, the dossier says they are thin laminated test panels called reedleaf by the Paven workshop. They are experimental samples, not approved building components. Each panel carries an inked identifier that remains attached throughout the sequence. The dossier mentions laminated layers and proposes unequal moisture uptake across those layers as a possible explanation, but it gives no materials list, no dimensions, no construction specification, no adhesive formulation, and no account of how the panels were made or obtained. It also does not identify the unresponsive panels as a separate manufacturing batch.

The stated purpose is to compare the panels’ shape after movement between a damp room and a dry room. The notebook’s working explanation is unequal moisture uptake across the laminated layers, but it treats temperature drift and handling effects as unresolved alternatives and warns that judging an edge from a photograph can depend on camera position. On reversibility, it records that 6 responsive panels flattened and then raised their edges again during a damp-to-dry-to-damp sequence, but it says this supports reversibility only over the observed two-day interval. It explicitly says the team did not test repeated cycling over several weeks."}

{"reply":"(1) Two-day timeline, as far as the dossier actually goes. Day 1 — September 2019, exact date not given: 24 foldglass panels, each with an inked identifier, are in 4 shallow trays, 6 per tray. The observers keep the tray supports and room lamps unchanged, and they move whole trays rather than spray individual panels. The general recorded procedure is that after 3 hours in each room, each identified sample is photographed beside the same square-grid reference card. The only specifically reported timed stage on the first day is the damp-room period. After that damp-room period, photographs show 16 panels with visibly raised free edges and 8 panels with no clear change. The dossier does not give the individual panel identifiers, does not state the starting room, does not state whether a dry-room period also occurred on day 1, and does not give a count of photographs. Day 2 — the next day: 7 panels described as responsive undergo a damp-to-dry-to-damp sequence. All 7 flatten and then raise their edges again in that order. The dossier does not say how the 7 were chosen; it gives no selection criteria and does not explicitly state that they were taken from the 16 raised panels, although that is a natural inference. It also does not separately restate the timing of the next-day sequence; the only timing anywhere in the dossier is 3 hours in each room, so applying that 3-hour rule to the next-day sequence is a deduction rather than an explicit statement. It does not list exactly which photographs were taken on day 2, though it says brief removal from a room for photography does not immediately erase the observed shape. Remaining panels: the dossier does not say what happened to the remaining panels on day 2. Arithmetically, 17 panels were not part of the described 7-panel next-day sequence, including the 8 unchanged panels, but the dossier gives no room placement, transfer, photograph, or outcome for them.

(2) Caveats, limitations, warnings, and explicitly noted absences: The square-grid reference card supplies a visual reference, not a reported angle measurement. The notebook does not identify the unresponsive samples as a separate manufacturing batch. Its comparison concerns edge shape, not the appearance of a new coating or a different ink label. The panels are experimental samples, not approved building components. The recorded sequence covers 2 days and supports reversibility only over that observed interval. Unequal moisture uptake across the laminated layers is proposed only as a working explanation. Room transfers alone do not isolate that mechanism from temperature drift or handling effects. Those alternatives are treated as unresolved. The notebook warns that judging an edge from a photograph can depend on camera position. Brief removal from a room for photography does not immediately erase the observed shape. No internal-layer microscopy was performed. No outdoor-weather exposure trial was conducted. The team did not test repeated cycling over several weeks. In addition, the dossier leaves several things unstated: panel identifiers, exact photograph counts, the selection method for the 7 panels, and the second-day handling of the remaining panels.

(3) Other dossier content beyond the main panel sequence: The dossier names Corda workshop and says it calls the thin laminated test panels foldglass. It names Maro Pell as the notebook author and gives the date September 2019. It mentions observers and a team, but does not name them. It mentions inked identifiers, shallow trays, tray supports, room lamps, whole-tray movement, and the same square-grid reference card. It includes the working explanation of unequal moisture uptake and the unresolved alternatives of temperature drift and handling effects. It does not record any other experiments, named people, panel names, materials specifications, dimensions, manufacturing steps, outdoor tests, microscopy, or repeated-cycling trials beyond what is already tied to the main two-day sequence."}

{"reply":"(1) Timeline: The dossier gives only a partial timeline, not a complete step-by-step log. It records 32 reedleaf panels in 4 shallow trays, with 8 panels per tray. Each panel has an inked identifier, but the identifiers are not listed. The observers keep tray supports and room lamps unchanged and move whole trays rather than individual panels. The stated timed practice is 3 hours in each room, followed by photographing each identified sample beside the same square-grid reference card. Photography involves brief removal from a room, and the dossier notes that this brief removal does not immediately erase the observed shape. The first specifically reported stage is after a damp-room period: photographs show 24 panels with visibly raised free edges and 8 panels with no clear change. The dossier does not state whether the 32 panels had a preceding dry-room placement on that day, nor does it enumerate every photograph or give individual panel identifiers. On the next day, 6 responsive panels undergo a damp-to-dry-to-damp sequence, meaning a damp placement, then a dry placement, then a return to damp. In that sequence, all 6 flatten and then raise their edges again, in that order. The dossier does not state how the 6 were chosen beyond being responsive; I can only infer that they came from the earlier responsive group, but that selection rule is not explicitly documented. It also does not say what happened to the remaining panels, including the 8 unchanged ones, on the second day.

(2) Caveats, limitations, warnings, and explicitly noted absences: the panels are experimental samples, not approved building components. The square-grid reference card supplies a visual reference, not a reported angle measurement. Eight panels show no clear change in the photographs. The notebook does not identify the unresponsive samples as a separate manufacturing batch. The comparison concerns edge shape, not the appearance of a new coating or a different ink label. Brief removal from a room for photography does not immediately erase the observed shape. The recorded sequence covers only 2 days and supports reversibility only over that observed interval. Unequal moisture uptake is presented only as a working explanation. Room transfers alone do not isolate that mechanism from temperature drift or handling effects. Judging an edge from a photograph can depend on camera position. There is no comparison of adhesive formulations. The team did not test repeated cycling over several weeks. No chemical-composition assay was performed.

(3) Other dossier content beyond the main panel sequence: the dossier names the Paven workshop and says it calls the thin laminated test panels reedleaf. It names Tessa Rin as the bench-notebook author and dates the notebook to November 2018. It mentions unnamed observers, trays, tray supports, room lamps, inked identifiers, and the square-grid reference card as part of the setting. It includes the working explanation of unequal moisture uptake across laminated layers and the caution about camera position. It does not record any other experiments, any other named people, any additional observations, or any further results beyond the panel sequence."}
